    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d162">

                <pb facs="0293" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">367n.1121 Hanovich, Israel/14: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Jerusalem</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Heizer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Jerusalem</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-05-21T10:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 21, 1924—10
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 21—9:23 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to Department’s telegram May 19, 3 p.m.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d162-f1"
                    /> Hanovich released on bail. Court now pressing him to pay fine which he
                    refuses reiterating at every step from the beginning his citizenship American
                    and demanding to be heard in the Consular Court. May be imprisoned again unless
                    he pays. Should a protest be made by me to the Government of Palestine in all
                    cases heard in the courts locally versus American citizens who refuse to submit
                    in writing to local jurisdiction?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Heizer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
